---------------------------------\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0 | Semantic Versioning (https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"MUST NOT be modified\"}); Total lines: 224\nL62: ## Semantic Versioning Specification (SemVer)\nL63: \nL64: The key words “MUST”, “MUST NOT”, “REQUIRED”, “SHALL”, “SHALL NOT”, “SHOULD”, “SHOULD NOT”, “RECOMMENDED”, “MAY”, and “OPTIONAL” in this document are to be interpreted as described in cite43†RFC 2119†tools.ietf.org .\nL65: \nL66:   1. Software using Semantic Versioning MUST declare a public API. This API could be declared in the code itself or exist strictly in documentation. However it is done, it SHOULD be precise and comprehensive.\nL67:   2. A normal version number MUST take the form X.Y.Z where X, Y, and Z are non-negative integers, and MUST NOT contain leading zeroes. X is the major version, Y is the minor version, and Z is the patch version. Each element MUST increase numerically. For instance: 1.9.0 -> 1.10.0 -> 1.11.0.\nL68: \nL69:   3. Once a versioned package has been released, the contents of that version MUST NOT be modified. Any modifications MUST be released as a new version.\nL70:   4. Major version zero (0.y.z) is for initial development. Anything MAY change at any time. The public API SHOULD NOT be considered stable.\nL71: \nL72:   5. Version 1.0.0 defines the public API. The way in which the version number is incremented after this release is dependent on this public API and how it changes.\nL73: \nL74:   6. Patch version Z (x.y.Z | x > 0) MUST be incremented if only backward compatible bug fixes are introduced. A bug fix is defined as an internal change that fixes incorrect behavior.\nL75:   7. Minor version Y (x.Y.z | x > 0) MUST be incremented if new, backward compatible functionality is introduced to the public API. It MUST be incremented if any public API functionality is marked as deprecated. It MAY be incremented if substantial new functionality or improvements are introduced within the private code. It MAY include patch level changes. Patch version MUST be reset to 0 when minor version is incremented.\nL76:   8. Major version X (X.y.z | X > 0) MUST be incremented if any backward incompatible changes are introduced to the public API. It MAY also include minor and patch level changes. Patch and minor versions MUST be reset to 0 when major version is incremented.\nL77:   9. A pre-release version MAY be denoted by appending a hyphen and a series of dot separated identifiers immediately following the patch version. Identifiers MUST comprise only ASCII alphanumerics and hyphens [0-9A-Za-z-]. Identifiers MUST NOT be empty. Numeric identifiers MUST NOT include leading zeroes. Pre-release versions have a lower precedence than the associated normal version.\nL78: A pre-release version indicates that the version is unstable and might not satisfy the intended compatibility requirements as denoted by its associated normal version. Examples: 1.0.0-alpha, 1.0.0-alpha.1, 1.0.0-0.3.7, 1.0.0-x.7.z.92, 1.0.0-x-y-z.--.\nL79:   10. Build metadata MAY be denoted by appending a plus sign and a series of dot separated identifiers immediately following the patch or pre-release version. Identifiers MUST comprise only ASCII alphanumerics and hyphens [0-9A-Za-z-]. Identifiers MUST NOT be empty. Build metadata MUST be ignored when determining version precedence. Thus two versions that differ only in the build metadata, have the same precedence.\nL80: Examples: 1.0.0-alpha+001, 1.0.0+20130313144700, 1.0.0-beta+exp.sha.5114f85, 1.0.0+21AF26D3----117B344092BD.\nL81:   11. Precedence refers to how versions are compared to each other when ordered.\nL82: \nL83:     1. Precedence MUST be calculated by separating the version into major, minor, patch and pre-release identifiers in that order (Build metadata does not figure into precedence).\nL84: \nL85:     2. Precedence is determined by the first difference when comparing each of these identifiers from left to right as follows: Major, minor, and patch versions are always compared numerically.\nL86: \nL87: Example: 1.0.0 < 2.0.0 < 2.1.0 < 2.1.1.\nL88:     3. When major, minor, and patch are equal, a pre-release version has lower precedence than a normal version:\nL89: \nL90: Example: 1.0.0-alpha < 1.0.0.\nL91: \nL92:     4. Precedence for two pre-release versions with the same major, minor, and patch version MUST be determined by comparing each dot separated identifier from left to right until a difference is found as follows:\nL93: \nL94:       1. Identifiers consisting of only digits are compared numerically.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWorkflow message passing - PHP SDK | Temporal Documentation (https://docs.temporal.io/develop/php/message-passing)\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"Signal\"}); Redirected to URL: https://docs.temporal.io/develop/php/workflows/message-passing; Total lines: 1168\nL145: Note that name parameter of Workflow method attributes can be used to specify name of Workflow, Signal and Query types. If name is not specified the short name of the Workflow interface is used.\nL146: \nL147: In the preceding code the `#[WorkflowMethod(name)]` is not specified, thus the Workflow Type defaults to `\"FileProcessingWorkflow\"`.\nL148: ### Handle a Signalcite56†​ L149: \nL150: Workflows listen for Signals by the Signal's name.\nL151: Use the `#[SignalMethod]` attribute to handle Signals in the Workflow interface:\nL152: \nL153:     `\nL154: \nL155:     use Temporal\\Workflow;\nL156: \nL157:     #[Workflow\\WorkflowInterface]\nL158: \nL159:     class YourWorkflow\nL160: \nL161:     {\nL162: \nL163:         private bool $value;\nL164: \nL165:         #[Workflow\\WorkflowMethod]\nL166: \nL167:         public function run()\nL168: \nL169:         {\nL170: \nL171:             yield Workflow::await(fn()=> $this->value);\nL172: \nL173:             return 'OK';\nL174: \nL175:         }\nL176: \nL177:         #[Workflow\\SignalMethod]\nL178:         public function setValue(bool $value)\nL179: \nL180:         {\nL181: \nL182:             $this->value = $value;\nL183: \nL184:         }\nL185: \nL186:     }\nL187: \nL188:     `\nL189: In the preceding example, the Workflow updates the protected value. The main Workflow coroutine waits for the value to change by using the `Workflow::await()` function.\nL190: ### Send a Signal from a Temporal Clientcite57†​ L191: \nL192: When a Signal is sent successfully from the Temporal Client, the cite58†WorkflowExecutionSignaled Event appears in the Event History of the Workflow that receives the Signal.\nL193: \nL194: To send a Signal to a Workflow Execution from a Client, call the Signal method, annotated with `#[SignalMethod]` in the Workflow interface, from the Client code.\nL195: To send a Signal to a Workflow, use `WorkflowClient->newWorkflowStub` or `WorkflowClient->newUntypedWorkflowStub`:\nL196: \nL197:     `\nL198: \nL199:     $workflow = $workflowClient->newWorkflowStub(YourWorkflow::class);\nL200: \nL201:     $run = $workflowClient->start($workflow);\nL202: \nL203:     // do something\nL204: \nL205:     $workflow->setValue(true);\nL206: \nL207:     assert($run->getValue() === true);\nL208: \nL209:     `\nL210: Use `WorkflowClient->newRunningWorkflowStub` or `WorkflowClient->newUntypedRunningWorkflowStub` with Workflow Id to send Signals to already running Workflows.\nL211: \nL212:     `\nL213: \nL214:     $workflow = $workflowClient->newRunningWorkflowStub(YourWorkflow::class, 'workflowID');\nL215: \nL216:     $workflow->setValue(true);\nL217: \nL218:     `\nL219: \nL220: See cite56†Handle Signal for details on how to handle Signals in a Workflow.\nL221: ### Send a Signal from a Workflowcite59†​ L222: \nL223: A Workflow can send a Signal to another Workflow, in which case it's called an External Signal.\nL224: \nL225: When an External Signal is sent:\nL226: \nL227:   * A cite60†SignalExternalWorkflowExecutionInitiated Event appears in the sender's Event History.\nL228:   * A cite58†WorkflowExecutionSignaled Event appears in the recipient's Event History.\nL229: To send Signal to a Workflow use `WorkflowClient`->`newWorkflowStub` or `WorkflowClient`->`newUntypedWorkflowStub`:\nL230: \nL231:     `\nL232: \nL233:     $workflow = $workflowClient->newWorkflowStub(YourWorkflow::class);\nL234: \nL235:     $run = $workflowClient->start($workflow);\nL236: \nL237:     // do something\nL238: \nL239:     $workflow->setValue(true);\nL240: \nL241:     assert($run->getValue() === true);\nL242: \nL243:     `\nL244: Use `WorkflowClient`->`newRunningWorkflowStub` or `WorkflowClient->newUntypedRunningWorkflowStub` with Workflow Id to send Signals to a running Workflow.\nL245: \nL246:     `\nL247: \nL248:     $workflow = $workflowClient->newRunningWorkflowStub(YourWorkflow::class, 'workflowID');\nL249: \nL250:     $workflow->setValue(true);\nL251: \nL252:     `\nL253: ### Use Signal-With-Startcite61†​ L254: \nL255: Signal-With-Start is used from the Client. It takes a Workflow Id, Workflow arguments, a Signal name, and Signal arguments.\nL256: \nL257: If there's a Workflow running with the given Workflow Id, it will be signaled. If there isn't, a new Workflow will be started and immediately signaled.\nL258: In cases where you may not know if a Workflow is running, and want to send a Signal to it, use `startwithSignal`. If a running Workflow exists, the `startwithSignal` API sends the Signal. If there is no running Workflow, the API starts a new Workflow Run and delivers the Signal to it.\nL259: \nL260:     `\nL261: \nL262:     $workflow = $workflowClient->newWorkflowStub(YourWorkflow::class);\nL263: \nL264:     $run = $workflowClient->startWithSignal(\nL265: \nL266:         $workflow,\nL267: \nL268:         'setValue',\nL269:         [true], // signal arguments\nL270: \nL271:         [] // start arguments\nL272: \nL273:     );\nL274: \nL275:     `\nL276: ## Develop with Queriescite62†​ L277: \nL278: A cite55†Query is a synchronous operation that is used to get the state of a Workflow Execution.\nL279: ### Define a Querycite63†​ L280: \nL281: A Query has a name and can have arguments.\nL282: \nL283:   * The name, also called a Query type, is a string.\nL284:   * The arguments must be cite54†serializable .\nL285: \nL286: Workflows can answer synchronous cite55†Queries and receive cite52†Signals .\nL287: \nL288: All interface methods must have one of the following attributes:\nL289:   * #[WorkflowMethod] indicates an entry point to a Workflow. It contains parameters that specify timeouts and a Task Queue name. Required parameters (such as `executionStartToCloseTimeoutSeconds`) that are not specified through the attribute must be provided at runtime.\nL290:   * #[SignalMethod] indicates a method that reacts to external signals. It must have a `void` return type.\nL291:   * #[QueryMethod] indicates a method that reacts to synchronous query requests. It must have a non `void` return type.\nL292: > It is possible (though not recommended for usability reasons) to annotate concrete class implementation.\nL293: \nL294: You can have more than one method with the same attribute (except #[WorkflowMethod]).\nL295: For example:\nL296: \nL297:     `\nL298: \nL299:     use Temporal\\Workflow\\WorkflowInterface;\nL300: \nL301:     use Temporal\\Workflow\\WorkflowMethod;\nL302: \nL303:     use Temporal\\Workflow\\SignalMethod;\nL304: \nL305:     use Temporal\\Workflow\\QueryMethod;\nL306: \nL307:     #[WorkflowInterface]\nL308: \nL309:     interface FileProcessingWorkflow\nL310: \nL311:     {\nL312: \nL313:         #[WorkflowMethod]\nL314: \nL315:         public function processFile(Argument $args);\nL316: \nL317:         #[QueryMethod(\"history\")]\nL318: \nL319:         public function getHistory(): array;\nL320: \nL321:         #[QueryMethod(\"status\")]\nL1045: ### Set a Dynamic Signalcite122†​ L1046: \nL1047: A Dynamic Signal in Temporal is a Signal that is invoked dynamically at runtime if no other Signal with the same input is registered. Use cite123†`Workflow::registerDynamicSignal()`†php.temporal.io to set a dynamic Signal handler.\nL1048: The Signal Handler parameters must accept a `string` name and cite121†`ValuesInterface`†php.temporal.io for the arguments.\nL1114:   * cite51†Develop with Signals L1115:     * cite53†Define a Signal L1116:     * cite56†Handle a Signal L1117:     * cite57†Send a Signal from a Temporal Client L1118:     * cite59†Send a Signal from a Workflow L1119:     * cite61†Use Signal-With-Start L1120:   * cite62†Develop with Queries L1121:     * cite63†Define a Query L1122:     * cite64†Handle a Query L1123:     * cite66†Send a Query L1124:   * cite67†Develop with Updates L1125:     * cite69†Define Update L1126:     * cite71†Handle Update L1127:     * cite72†Validate Update L1128:     * cite74†Send Update from a Client L1129:   * cite85†Message handler patterns "
